Derived work
------------------------------------------------------------------------

This project began as the `custom_hardware` example from the LiveKit ESP32
client SDK:

    https://github.com/livekit/client-sdk-esp32
    Copyright LiveKit, Inc. — Apache License 2.0

The upstream example targets a Waveshare development board with a
touchscreen, two audio chips, and a separate power-management IC. It does
not run on the HA-TOYMD module as-is. The following files were modified or
rewritten by GMIC AI Inc. to bring LiveKit up on this hardware:

  main/board.c    Rewritten. The upstream implementation drives a different
                  board: two codecs on different pins, plus a PMIC that does
                  not exist here. This module has a single ES8311 handling
                  both capture and playback.

  main/media.c    Capture path changed. Upstream routes the microphone
                  through the on-device AEC front end, which expects a
                  4-channel TDM stream with a hardware reference channel.
                  This module has one microphone on a 2-channel bus; feeding
                  the AEC front end from it overflows its ring buffer and
                  tears the connection down after roughly 27 seconds.
                  Capture is now taken straight from the codec, and echo
                  cancellation is left to the application side.

  main/main.c     Startup banner added, so a board flashed without
                  credentials explains what is missing instead of failing
                  silently.

  main/board.h    Pin map for this module. The values were recovered by
  (pin values)    reading the GPIO routing registers out of the running
                  factory firmware over the chip's built-in USB-JTAG port,
                  then verified on hardware.

  sdkconfig.defaults, partitions.csv
                  Adjusted for 4 MB flash and 2 MB quad PSRAM.

Everything else is upstream, unchanged, and pulled by the ESP-IDF component
manager at build time. See dependencies.lock for the exact versions.
